---
import GuideLayout from '../../components/GuideLayout.astro';

const title = 'How to Set Up 2FA on Twitch (Authenticator App)';
const description =
  'Enable Twitch two-factor authentication with an authenticator app. Twitch requires a phone first; then scan the QR and generate live TOTP codes in your browser.';
---

<GuideLayout
  serviceName="Twitch"
  title={title}
  description={description}
  path="/guides/twitch/"
  datePublished="2026-11-18"
  dateModified="2026-11-23"
  lastReviewed="2026-11-23"
  reviewedVia="browser"
  sources={[
    {
      label: 'Twitch Help — Setting up Two-Factor Authentication (2FA)',
      href: 'https://help.twitch.tv/s/article/two-factor-authentication?language=en_US',
      kind: 'official',
    },
    {
      label: 'Twitch Help — How to Change Your 2FA Phone Number on Twitch',
      href: 'https://help.twitch.tv/s/article/authy-faq?language=en_US',
      kind: 'official',
    },
    {
      label: 'RFC 6238 — TOTP: Time-Based One-Time Password Algorithm',
      href: 'https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc6238',
      kind: 'independent',
    },
  ]}
  intro="Twitch will only turn on two-factor authentication after you verify your email and a phone number, and the phone comes first: the authenticator app is added on top, not instead. Enabling 2FA also creates an Authy account tied to that number, which is why losing the number is a slower problem than losing a phone."
  minutes={5}
  params={['6 digits', '30 s period', 'SHA-1']}
  ctaTitle="A stream key is a password you cannot rotate quietly"
  ctaBody="A hijacked Twitch account can leak your stream key, change payouts, and impersonate you to your community. Save the setup secret and the backup codes — the secret regenerates codes, but only the backup codes and Authy's phone change form get you back in when the phone is gone."
  deepDive={{
    heading: "Twitch details that trip people up",
    blocks: [
      {
        title: 'The phone number is a gate, a backup and an Authy account',
        body: 'Three things are true of that number at once. Twitch requires it before 2FA can be switched on. The interface labels it “Text Message (Backup)” once the app is enrolled. And enabling 2FA creates an Authy account tied to it — for every user, whichever app they chose. That last part is what surprises people: if you lose the number, Twitch sends you to Authy’s phone change form, which Twitch says takes 24–48 business hours, not to Twitch support.',
      },
      {
        title: 'Your stream key deserves its own protection',
        body: 'The stream key is a credential that lets software broadcast as your channel. It is stored in OBS or your encoder, not in the authenticator. If you suspect a leak, reset the stream key in the Twitch dashboard immediately — 2FA protects account login, not an already-copied key.',
      },
      {
        title: '2FA is required for monetization, and it pays in emotes',
        body: 'Twitch requires two-factor authentication to get started as an Affiliate or Partner, and it unlocks six exclusive emotes. The emotes are removed whenever SMS or TOTP is disabled, including when you switch from one authenticator app to another, and they return when you finish setup again. So a payout and an emote set can both be gated on the factor you are setting up here.',
      },
    ],
  }}
  steps={[
    {
      title: 'Open Twitch security settings',
      body: 'Sign in at twitch.tv/settings/security — the help centre links that page directly. You can also reach it from your profile picture → “Settings” → “Security and Privacy”.',
    },
    {
      title: 'Verify your email, then start setup',
      body: 'Twitch requires a verified email address before it will begin. Select “Set Up Two-Factor Authentication”, then “Enable 2FA”.',
    },
    {
      title: 'Add the phone number first',
      body: 'Enter the number you control — Twitch advises against VoIP numbers — then the 7-digit code it texts you. This step is not optional: Twitch requires SMS verification before any authenticator app, and on the mobile app SMS is the only method available.',
    },
    {
      title: 'Add the authenticator app',
      body: 'Once the number is verified, Twitch offers the authenticator app: scan the QR code with your app. If your screen also exposes a setup key, copy it instead. To use this browser generator, screenshot the QR code and paste the image into it — decoding happens on your device.',
    },
    {
      title: 'Generate the code and submit it',
      body: 'A live 6-digit code appears in the generator. Paste it into Twitch’s verification field and submit. Twitch also lets you scan the same QR with a second app or device, which is a legitimate backup rather than a duplicate.',
    },
    {
      title: 'Save the backup codes',
      body: 'Twitch issues backup codes during setup; save them now. They are the documented way back in if you still have account access but have lost the phone. Then store the setup secret in your password manager so you can generate codes on any device.',
    },
  ]}
  faqs={[
    {
      q: 'Does Twitch use standard TOTP?',
      a: 'Yes. Twitch accepts any app that supports TOTP, and its help centre names Authy, Google Authenticator, Microsoft Authenticator, LastPass and Duo Mobile as examples. The same secret works in this browser generator.',
    },
    {
      q: 'Why does Twitch demand a phone number for 2FA?',
      a: 'Because SMS verification is a required first step, not an alternative. Twitch’s help centre states that SMS verification is always required before an authenticator app can be added, and that on the mobile app SMS is the only method. Enrolling also creates an Authy account linked to that number.',
    },
    {
      q: 'Can I stream without SMS after enabling the app?',
      a: 'You can use the authenticator for daily codes, but you cannot skip the phone: Twitch requires SMS verification first and lists the number as a backup method afterwards. If you still have access to the number and want to change it, use “Edit Two-Factor Authentication” → “Modify” next to “Text Message (Backup)”.',
    },
    {
      q: 'I lost the phone with my Twitch authenticator. What now?',
      a: 'If you saved a backup code, use it, then change the number from the security settings. If you cannot sign in at all, Twitch’s documented route is Authy’s phone change form, which Twitch says it processes within 24–48 business hours. Your saved setup secret gets you codes, but it does not move the number.',
    },
  ]}
/>
